  <HaltConditions>
    | 🛑 ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_PHASE_NOT_FOUND` | `sdd-task --phase` returns `ERR_CLI_SDD_TASK_PHASE_NOT_FOUND` — the phase ID from the orchestrator matches no row in Phases Overview |
    | `H_PHASE_ALREADY_DONE` | The `status=` field in `sdd-task --phase`'s header line is `[x]` and no `fix` reason was passed |
    | `H_RECON_DIVERGENCE` | Recon shows Target Files inconsistent with the phase plan |
    | `H_MISSING_RULE_FILE` | A rule cited in the phase's `READ rules:` line does not resolve |
    | `H_INPUT_HANDOFF_MISSING` | A phase Input cites a prior phase whose Handoff is absent from `sdd-task --phase`'s `[HANDOFF]` block in the current Round |
    | `H_OUT_OF_PHASE_WRITE` | A write is required outside Target Files, a deletion outside Deleted Files, or a ticket write outside `AX_TICKET_WRITE_SCOPE` |
    | `H_THIRD_PARTY_API_UNVERIFIABLE` | `config` kind: ToolDocAdapter unsupported/ambiguous, installed version unavailable, or its permitted evidence is insufficient |
    | `H_VERIFICATION_FAIL` | Verification evidence is `RECOVERABLE_TECHNICAL`; the orchestrator owns bounded retry/exhaustion |
    | `H_TECHNICAL_REPLAN` | Missing config/package/gate/CREATE target, target mismatch, tool diagnostic, or task-decomposition defect — report `RECOVERABLE_TECHNICAL` with evidence and a bounded plan to the orchestrator |
    | `H_SPEC_GOAL_CONFLICT` | A technical repair contradicts an approved spec/BDD goal — report `SPEC_GOAL_CONFLICT` with the exact conflicting refs |
    | `H_EXTERNAL_AUTHORITY_REQUIRED` | Completion requires permission or external authority unavailable to the worker — report `EXTERNAL_AUTHORITY_REQUIRED` with evidence |
  </HaltConditions>

  <OutcomeRouting>
    `H_VERIFICATION_FAIL` and `H_TECHNICAL_REPLAN` return `RECOVERABLE_TECHNICAL`;
    `H_SPEC_GOAL_CONFLICT` returns `SPEC_GOAL_CONFLICT`; `H_EXTERNAL_AUTHORITY_REQUIRED` returns
    `EXTERNAL_AUTHORITY_REQUIRED`. The orchestrator owns every NEXT transition.
  </OutcomeRouting>

  <ContextExpectation>
    Dispatch contains only this directive path, ticket, phase, kind + reason (`initial` / `fix:
    F-NNN` / `resume`), and 1-3 context lines the ticket lacks. It does NOT paste the read-manifest, gates, or
    prior Handoff — STEP_1 gets all of that directly, as the first action of this phase, by calling
    `sdd-task` itself. A tool call made fresh at the start of THIS run cannot go stale between
    dispatch and execution — the concern a pasted-prompt approach had on a retry.

    This activation may land in a session that already ran a prior phase for this same ticket's
    spec (the orchestrator's `AX_WORKER_SESSION_REUSE`) — continuing a session is a context
    optimization on the orchestrator's side, never a reason to skip or shortcut STEP_1 below. Run
    STEP_1 (`sdd-task --phase`) fresh regardless, and do not rely on memory of the previous task —
    this task's own state lives entirely in what that call returns, not in what an earlier phase in
    this session remembered.

    Per `AX_READ_PER_MANIFEST`: read EXACTLY the manifest the tool hands you, nothing beyond it. Pull
    each cited block by anchor (`sdd-extract <file> <NAME>`, or `sdd-extract <file>#<anchor>` when the
    manifest already prints the combined form), not whole files. Something needed but
    absent from the manifest → emit a `discovery` line and surface it; keep the read within the
    manifest.

    **Tool reference** — this tested table is the complete mechanical surface for the phase. Do not
    call `--help`; each row gives the exact grammar and an example:

    | Tool | When | Call |
    |---|---|---|
    | `sdd-task <ticket> --phase P<N>` | FIRST action, every phase, every retry — objective, gates + satisfy-hints, exit, phase-scoped READ/CREATE lifecycle manifest, prior Handoff | `npx gennady sdd-task <ticket> --phase P2` |
    | `sdd-extract <file> <NAME>` · `sdd-extract <file>#<anchor>` | Pull ONE cited block instead of a whole file — `<NAME>` is a `SECTION_NAME` (`PHASE_P1`, `BDD`, `VERIFICATION`, …), two args; a markdown heading anchor is the combined `path#lower-dashed-slug` form the read-manifest prints | `npx gennady sdd-extract <ticket> PHASE_P1` · `npx gennady sdd-extract specs/app/greeting/greeting.spec.md#module-contracts` |
    | `sdd-log <ticket-path\|Task-ID> line --content-file <payload> --phase <PhaseID>` | Semantic event; agent writes free text with the file-write tool, never shell argv | `npx gennady sdd-log <ticket-path\|Task-ID> line --content-file .claude/tmp/TSK-x-P2-log.txt --phase P2` |
    | `sdd-log <ticket> phase <PhaseID> [--content-file <payload>]` | Fresh phase has no payload; a `fix` re-run file contains only the `— re-run:` suffix | `npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> phase P2 --content-file .claude/tmp/TSK-x-P2-log.txt` |
    | `sdd-log <ticket> handoff --content-file <payload> --phase <PhaseID>` | Typed `HANDOFF_FORMAT`, file-write then one-shot consume | `npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> handoff --content-file .claude/tmp/TSK-x-P2-handoff.txt --phase P2` |
    | `sdd-log <ticket> blocker --payload-file <json> --phase <PhaseID>` | Strict JSON keys `reason`,`axiom`,`unblock`; opens phase-owned resolution trail | `npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> blocker --payload-file .claude/tmp/TSK-x-P2-blocker.json --phase P2` |
    | `sdd-log <ticket> resolved --content-file <payload> --phase <PhaseID>` | Paired blocker close from literal remediation file | `npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> resolved --content-file .claude/tmp/TSK-x-P2-log.txt --phase P2` |
    | `sdd-check --task <ticket>` | A point re-check for the mechanical verdict after a fix, instead of re-deriving it by hand | `npx gennady sdd-check --task <ticket>` |
    | `sdd-verify --task <ticket-path> --phase <PhaseID>` | The complete STEP_5 transaction — derives context, repairs, runs foundation + applicable §5 rows once, writes the receipt | `npx gennady sdd-verify --task specs/app/app.task.TSK-1.md --phase P2` |
    | `sdd-verify --profile full` | Global read-only full gate; owned by dispatched audit, not phase execution | `npx gennady sdd-verify --profile full` |
    | `gennady yagni` | Runs INSIDE `sdd-verify --profile full` only (once per group, at group close); on `ERR_CLI_YAGNI_UNDERUSED` it prints a ready-to-paste `- **Usage Waiver:** <reason>` block — paste verbatim, then log it (`AX_USAGE_WAIVER_DISCIPLINE`) | standalone only for a quick iteration: `npx gennady yagni .` |
    | ticket coverage reader | Only in the declared `Coverage Owner Phase`; its `sdd-verify` call produces once then executes the one §5 Role=`coverage` reader — command owner may be Node/iOS/Android/Go | internal to the canonical phase call |

    `sdd-session open` has already created the regular non-symlink `.claude/tmp/` boundary. For every
    agent-produced free-text payload above, use the file-write tool (never shell echo/redirection/
    heredoc/escaping) to write the exact deterministic filename shown, then call the literal command.
    The CLI validates UTF-8/size/path, treats quotes/`$()`/backticks/newlines only as data, and deletes
    exactly that file after success. A failed append leaves it for correction; resolve any cleanup
    warning before handoff. Consumed scratch leaves phase source-state unchanged; never blanket-ignore
    other `.claude/tmp/` mutations.

    Follow the next action printed by a failed gate/tool. If none is actionable, return
    `RECOVERABLE_TECHNICAL` with that diagnostic as evidence; if execution requires an unavailable
    permission, return `EXTERNAL_AUTHORITY_REQUIRED`. The orchestrator owns the transition.
  </ContextExpectation>

  <PhaseProcedure>
    <Step id="STEP_1_GET_PHASE_CONTEXT">
      <Goal>First action, every phase, every retry: get the manifest from the tool, not from a pasted prompt.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Run phaseContext ToolCall below. Its output IS the phase's working
        context — objective, gates with a satisfy-hint, exit criterion, the phase-scoped
        lifecycle manifest (`READ files` are existing inputs; `CREATE files` are absent write
        reservations and MUST NOT be read before creation), and a `[HANDOFF]` block quoting every completed prior phase's verbatim
        `**Handoff →**` line (absent → this is the first phase). Follow this output for the rest of
        the phase; do not separately open Meta, Phases Overview, or another phase's body — the tool
        already filtered all of that. `ERR_CLI_SDD_TASK_PHASE_NOT_FOUND` → `H_PHASE_NOT_FOUND`; the
        header line's `status=[x]` with no `fix` reason from the dispatch → `H_PHASE_ALREADY_DONE`;
        an Input that should cite a prior phase but has no matching `[HANDOFF]` entry →
        `H_INPUT_HANDOFF_MISSING`.
        <ToolCall owner="phase-worker" result="phaseContext">npx gennady sdd-task <ticket> --phase <PhaseID></ToolCall> Inputs <ticket>, <PhaseID>: <ticket> and <PhaseID>=exact activation values supplied by execute STEP_2. Use phaseContext.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_1B_RESUME_OR_START">
      <Goal>Detect a prior aborted attempt at THIS phase before writing or logging anything.</Goal>
      <Action>
        `sdd-task --phase` shows prior PHASES' Handoff, never this phase's OWN Execution Log entries
        — check those separately: sectionExtract ToolCall below and look for this
        phase's heading in the current Round.

        <LogicSwitch on="this phase's own Execution Log entries, current Round">
        - WHEN a `#### <PhaseID>` block already exists WITHOUT a closing `DONE` + Handoff -> a prior
          attempt at this exact phase was interrupted (crash, context limit, killed process — not a
          formal `fix` re-run, which always closes with `DONE`). Read its logged lines in order;
          every already-logged `intro` / `decision` / `verified` line is done semantic work —
          do not redo it or re-log it, and do not re-open files it already reports created unless
          STEP_2 recon finds them missing. A prior receipt is replaced by STEP_5 so it attests the
          resumed attempt's final bytes. Do NOT call `sdd-log
          <ticket> phase <PhaseID>` again — the header already exists; resume by appending the next
          event line into that same block, continuing from where the log stopped.
        - WHEN no such open block exists (fresh phase, or the only block present is a prior Round's
          closed `— re-run: <reason>` attempt that already ends in `DONE` + Handoff) -> fresh start:
          without a fix reason run phaseLog ToolCall below. On a formal fix
          re-run, write exact `— re-run: <reason>` to `.claude/tmp/<task-id>-<phase-id>-log.txt`, then
          run phaseLog ToolCall below
          (`AX_TICKET_WRITE_SCOPE` — the tool is the sole writer, never a manual `Edit`).
        </LogicSwitch>
        <ToolCall owner="phase-worker" result="sectionExtract">npx gennady sdd-extract <ticket> EXECUTION_LOG</ToolCall> Use sectionExtract.
        <ToolCall owner="phase-worker" result="phaseLog">npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> phase <PhaseID></ToolCall> Use phaseLog.
        <ToolCall owner="phase-worker" result="phaseLog">npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> phase <PhaseID> --content-file .claude/tmp/<task-id>-<phase-id>-log.txt</ToolCall> Use phaseLog.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_2_NARROW_RECON">
      <Goal>Verify ground truth before writing — log only on divergence.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Per `AX_NARROW_RECON`: log a recon line ONLY on divergence; when state matches the plan, stay
        silent. On divergence write exact `discovery <fact>` to the phase log payload, then run
        eventLog ToolCall below;
        if it breaks the phase contract → `H_RECON_DIVERGENCE`, else proceed.
        <ToolCall owner="phase-worker" result="eventLog">npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> line --content-file .claude/tmp/<task-id>-<phase-id>-log.txt --phase <PhaseID></ToolCall> Inputs <ticket>, <task-id>, <phase-id>, <PhaseID>: file-write of literal divergence observed here. Use eventLog.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3_LOAD_RULES">
      <Goal>Internalize the rules governing this phase. No log line.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Read the rule files named on STEP_1's `READ rules:` line (`AX_RULES_LOAD_FROM_PHASE_BLOCK` +
        `AX_READ_PER_MANIFEST`) — the bounded set already filtered by phase kind + Target Files. A
        cited file that fails to resolve → `H_MISSING_RULE_FILE`. Skip the `rules` log line — the
        manifest already declares them.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3B_TOOL_API">
      <Goal>`config` kind only: verify each configured third-party tool through the declared ecosystem's registered ToolDocAdapter (`AX_THIRD_PARTY_TOOL_CURRENT_API`). Any other kind skips this step.</Goal>
      <Action>
        <ToolDocAdapterRegistry>
          Activate only when STEP_1 `phaseContext.kind=config`; every other kind skips without a
          probe. Detect ecosystem + tool only from that phaseContext's Target Files, Spec Refs, and
          readiness evidence — never memory, repo-wide search, or filename guessing. Each adapter
          owns its version source, permitted local docs, and explicit remote policy:

          | Adapter | Version + local docs | Remote policy |
          |---|---|---|
          | `node/npm` | `node_modules/<pkg>/package.json#version`; that installed package root's `README*` and `CHANGELOG*` | only if local docs are insufficient, WebFetch the exact `homepage` or `repository.url` in that same package manifest |
          | `go` | unsupported — no registered adapter | none |
          | `ios/swift` | unsupported — no registered adapter | none |
          | `android/gradle` | unsupported — no registered adapter | none |

          An ambiguous ecosystem, unregistered tool, unsupported adapter, missing installed version,
          or insufficient permitted evidence → `H_THIRD_PARTY_API_UNVERIFIABLE`. Missing adapter,
          version, or persisted docs is `RECOVERABLE_TECHNICAL`; an exact external-access permission
          is `EXTERNAL_AUTHORITY_REQUIRED`. Return evidence and the bounded next action. Never guess, call `--help`,
          explore an undeclared cache, inspect tool internals, or access the network outside the
          selected adapter's remote policy.
        </ToolDocAdapterRegistry>

        BEFORE writing config, select and follow that adapter. Exact config specifics remain a
        decision-time infra-spec concern
        (`AX_RESEARCH_PERSISTED`, `infra.directive` STEP_3_TOOL_CHOICES), sourced from Spec Refs or a
        linked research artifact.
        Then write exact `verified <tool>@<version> <one-line summary>` to the phase log payload and
        run eventLog ToolCall below — audit requires this entry per config artifact.
        <ToolCall owner="phase-worker" result="eventLog">npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> line --content-file .claude/tmp/<task-id>-<phase-id>-log.txt --phase <PhaseID></ToolCall> Inputs <ticket>, <task-id>, <phase-id>, <PhaseID>: file-write of literal evidence gathered here. Use eventLog.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_4_EXECUTE">
      <Goal>Produce the phase artifacts in Target Files and remove every declared Deleted File.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Before any probe that needs scratch state, use deterministic files prefixed
        `.claude/tmp/<task-id>-<phase-id>-`; consume/remove each exact file before handoff. This
        boundary already exists from `sdd-session open`, so the worker never has to discover or
        request `/tmp`, `$TMPDIR`, a home-directory cache, or another permission boundary.
        Apply rules continuously; respect `AX_PHASE_SCOPE_LOCK`, `AX_FILE_HEADER_APPEND_ONLY`,
        `AX_INTRODUCED_DISCIPLINE`. `test` kind honors `AX_BDD_NAME_DISCIPLINE`; `config` kind applies
        the current-version API verified at STEP_3B (`AX_THIRD_PARTY_TOOL_CURRENT_API`). Dependency
        registry/install commands are allowed only when this phase is `bootstrap`, its Objective names
        adding that dependency, and Target Files contain `package.json` plus the active npm lock file.
        For one `<pkg>` at a time, run exactly `npm view <pkg> engines`, `npm view <pkg> version`, and
        `npm view <pkg> dist-tags`; no `--help` or other probe is needed. Then use exactly
        `npm install --save-dev --save-exact <pkg>@<verified-stable-version>` or
        `npm add --save-dev --save-exact <pkg>@<verified-stable-version>` for a dev tool; for a runtime
        dependency use exactly `npm install <pkg>@<verified-stable-version>` or
        `npm add <pkg>@<verified-stable-version>`. This
        works `AX_DEPENDENCY_ADDITION_CHECKLIST` — Node/module-system compatibility,
        pinning discipline, and release channel — without permitting any other npm/network command;
        non-bootstrap phases remain forbidden from adding dependencies. Depth of the
        work itself is public-release by default, not POC (`AX_RELEASE_READY_DEFAULT`) — error
        handling, edge cases, empty/loading states are part of THIS phase's Objective unless the
        ticket itself named POC/MVP. For each event, write exact content to the phase log payload and
        run eventLog ToolCall below
        as it happens (`AX_LIVE_LOG`, `AX_TICKET_WRITE_SCOPE` — the tool
        is the sole writer, never a manual `Edit`); a spec gap goes to an `insight` line the same way and leaves
        the spec untouched (`AX_SPEC_NEVER_EDITED`). A TICKET gap — the phase needs a decision the
        ticket does not cover — is not a spec gap and not a reason to stop and ask the operator:
        decide it yourself, then append a ticket-local Decision Log entry via a direct `Edit`
        (`AX_DEVIATION_SELF_RESOLVE`, `AX_TICKET_WRITE_SCOPE`) — one line, id `<ACR>-DL-N`
        (ticket-local, per `AX_DEVIATION_SELF_RESOLVE`), body shaped per `DECISION_LOG_ENTRY_FORMAT`:
        `<ACR>-DL-N <date> — <decision in one phrase> (почему: <compromise/criterion>; отвергнуто:
        <alternative — why not>; риск: <if accepted>)` — omit a clause entirely rather than writing it
        empty — and continue. A deletion is autonomous only when its exact path is already declared:
        use exact `unlink` for that entry and exact `rmdir` only for a declared directory after its
        declared contents are gone. Never use a glob/recursive delete or reinterpret a missing Target
        File as deletion; an undeclared deletion is `H_OUT_OF_PHASE_WRITE` and routes to re-planning.
        <ToolCall owner="phase-worker" result="eventLog">npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> line --content-file .claude/tmp/<task-id>-<phase-id>-log.txt --phase <PhaseID></ToolCall> Inputs <ticket>, <PhaseID>: file-write of one concrete event produced here. Use eventLog.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_5_VERIFY">
      <Goal>Let the CLI run and attest one complete mechanical phase-verification attempt.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Run verification ToolCall below
        (`AX_PERMITTED_BASH_COMMANDS`, `AX_VERIFICATION_BEFORE_HANDOFF`). This is the one mechanical owner: it derives profile, exact
        Target Files, owning spec and applicable §Verification rows from the ticket; performs exact-target
        repair through ordered formatter/project-linter/Gennady-contract adapters, each receiving only
        its applicable exact targets, under a runtime before/after write-zone (any outside mutation is listed, left intact and
        halts before foundation; `.git` metadata and installed `node_modules` are excluded tool-state), one foundation run, then those read-only extra rows sequentially under
        a zero-write boundary; and only on complete success
        atomically records the structured phase receipt in Execution Log. Do not reconstruct arguments,
        run a §5 row yourself, add `ver` attestations, or duplicate the command within one attempt. Required coverage keeps
        one producer followed by its read/check row; not-applicable invents neither. `setup` may honestly
        skip absent bootstrap bricks; code/test fail closed. The receipt is mechanical evidence, not the
        semantic Handoff in STEP_6.

        On failure, fix only inside this phase's Target Files (regardless of age), then start a new attempt
        with the same command. Restore/remove a tombstone only through its declared `Deleted Files` outcome;
        tombstones are never repair targets. If remediation belongs to another technical phase,
        edge, artifact owner, package/config/gate, or a missing CREATE target, return
        `RECOVERABLE_TECHNICAL` with exact evidence and bounded task/DAG/artifact edits. The
        orchestrator records the checkpoint, amends the technical plan, reruns deterministic checks,
        and redispatches; this worker keeps the declared write zone. An
        out-of-inventory export is removed or logged as `intro <name> ← <reason>` for audit backflow
        (`AX_CLOSED_WORLD_INVENTORY`); failure returns a typed outcome per `AX_HALT_VS_FAIL_DISTINCTION`;
        unsafe casts are removed or explicitly justified per `AX_NO_SILENT_ESCAPE_HATCH`.
        If the same in-zone verification failure remains after its fixes, or its required tooling is
        unavailable, return `RECOVERABLE_TECHNICAL`; a checked phase without a receipt is never an
        exit. The orchestrator alone classifies replan exhaustion after the recorded budget.
        Once the cause is actually gone, write exact remediation to the phase log payload and run
        resolutionLog ToolCall below into THIS SAME phase block, per `AX_BLOCKER_RESOLUTION_TRAIL`;
        logged by whoever resumes the ticket (this phase's own retry, or the orchestrator), never a manual `Edit`.
        Inputs <ticket>: STEP_1 ticket identity.
        <ToolCall owner="phase-worker" result="verification">npx gennady sdd-verify --task <ticket-path> --phase <PhaseID></ToolCall> Inputs <ticket-path>: <ticket-path>=canonical path returned by STEP_1; <PhaseID>=exact STEP_1 phase. Use verification.
        <ToolCall owner="phase-worker" result="resolutionLog">npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> resolved --content-file .claude/tmp/<task-id>-<phase-id>-log.txt --phase <PhaseID></ToolCall> Inputs <task-id>, <phase-id>: exact current task and phase from phaseContext. Use resolutionLog.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_6_EMIT_HANDOFF">
      <Goal>Close the phase block with a typed Handoff and DONE.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Append `<ts> DONE` via eventLog ToolCall below; append the
        the exact `HANDOFF_FORMAT` payload with the file-write tool to
        `.claude/tmp/<task-id>-<phase-id>-handoff.txt`, then append `**Handoff →**` via
        handoffLog ToolCall below per
        `HANDOFF_FORMAT` (typed payload,
        `AX_HANDOFF_TYPED`, `AX_TICKET_WRITE_SCOPE` — the tool is the sole writer for both lines, never
        a manual `Edit`, and this phase's OWN and ONLY write of them — the orchestrator that dispatched
        this phase reads this Handoff line back but never re-logs it, per its own logging-zone boundary)
        — its `deviations:` field lists the id of every Decision Log entry this phase
        added while self-resolving a ticket gap (`AX_DEVIATION_SELF_RESOLVE`; empty `[]` when none);
        flip this phase's Status in Phases Overview `[ ]` → `[x]` via a direct `Edit`
        (outside the Execution Log, `AX_TICKET_WRITE_SCOPE`); return the structured summary per
        `RETURN_SUMMARY_FORMAT` to the orchestrator.
        Inputs <ticket>, <PhaseID>: STEP_1 phase context.
        <ToolCall owner="phase-worker" result="eventLog">npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> line "DONE" --phase <PhaseID></ToolCall> Use eventLog.
        <ToolCall owner="phase-worker" result="handoffLog">npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> handoff --content-file .claude/tmp/<task-id>-<phase-id>-handoff.txt --phase <PhaseID></ToolCall> Use handoffLog.
      </Action>
    </Step>
  </PhaseProcedure>

